% performance-note: No source performance exists. The first recording should be a classroom demo made with participant consent; the Dave Brubeck Quartet recording is a listening reference only and is not a performance of this piece.
% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 6
% composition-elements: 5/4 metre grouped 3+2 by the words, the melody and the ostinato; eighth-note triplets; an anacrusis ("and") leading into bars 3 and 11; i-iv-V harmony in A minor (Am, Dm, E, the minor-key I-IV-V); named intervals (perfect fourths, major thirds, the ostinato's perfect fifths); two layers, a sung melody over an instrumental root-and-fifth ostinato; ABA' form
% composition-listen: The Dave Brubeck Quartet — "Take Five" (composed by Paul Desmond), on the album Time Out (Columbia, 1959)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.davebrubeck.com/dave-brubecks-time-out
% composition-bridge: Feel 5/4 as a repeating grouping, then hear how a solo can range freely while the ostinato stays dependable.
% composition-context: Paul Desmond composed "Take Five" for the Dave Brubeck Quartet. This original 5/4 study uses a different melody, harmony and grouping material.
% composition-distance: No riff, piano vamp, chord progression, solo language, lyric or drum pattern from "Take Five" is copied; the ostinato is a plain root-and-fifth walk, and the form is ABA', not the reference's; credit Desmond as composer in the lesson.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"1-4","role":"body-percussion group","prompt":"Clap which grouping?","choices":["3+2 with the song","2+3 against it","five even claps"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"7-8","role":"bells or recorder soloist","prompt":"Fill the two silent beats","choices":["a rising perfect fourth","a falling perfect fifth","a major third"],"replyBars":1}
% dossier-function: original sung listening-bridge song for Grade 6, a counting song that makes 3+2 audible: the class sings the melody, a small group plays the ostinato, a body-percussion group chooses a grouping, and a soloist answers with a named interval in the rests
% dossier-elements: twelve bars in 5/4, every bar grouped 3+2; A minor with G sharp as leading note, pitch set D-E-F-G#-A-B-C-D-E; melody D4-E5, ostinato A3-B4; A (bars 1-4): quarter, two eighths, quarter, then a half note or a half rest (bars 1, 2, 4), and five counted quarters in bar 3, with an eighth-note anacrusis "and" at the end of bar 2; B (bars 5-8): eighth-note triplets on beats 1 and 4 of bar 5 and beat 1 of bars 6 and 7, half notes on beats 4-5, the harmony moving Dm to E at the 3+2 split of bar 7, a half rest in bar 8; A' (bars 9-12) repeats A with a new cadence; Am-Dm-E (i-iv-V) only; ABA' form; intervals named in the lesson: E-A and B-E perfect fourths, C-E, F-A and E-G# major thirds, and the ostinato's A-E, D-A and E-B perfect fifths
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 104 is the target; begin at 80 and count "ONE two three FOUR five" aloud before singing. Rehearsal key A minor; the melody's written range D4-E5 sits inside the Grade 4-8 voice range; against the young-unison C4-D5 range only the E5s (bars 1, 3, 4, 6, 9, 11, 12) lie above, so transpose down a tone to G minor if a class strains. Teach in steps: speak the words in rhythm while patting 3+2; sing A; teach the ostinato to a small group on bells, xylophone, ukulele roots or keyboard; sing A over the ostinato; teach B's triplets by speaking "some-one's a-sleep"; put ABA' together; add the body-percussion choice and the soloist's interval reply in bars 4 and 8.
% dossier-accompaniment: The ostinato (second voice) and the Am-Dm-E chords are newly authored. The page's pop band was chosen as a straight-eighth, non-jazz accompaniment so that the piece does not imitate the Quartet's swing; how it handles 5/4 must be checked in a trial, and the band can be muted so the class ostinato carries the metre. Do not play the reference's piano vamp or drum pattern under this song.
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record about five lit windows on a street at night; every melody note has one syllable, and the 3+2 grouping is in the words ("Three on the top floor, two by the door"). Sung melody; the ostinato is instrumental. No words come from the reference, which is instrumental, or from any later vocal version of it.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed range, metre and teaching constraints. Not traditional, not collected from any community and not modelled on the reference's melody, vamp, harmony or form; the Dave Brubeck Quartet recording is a listening bridge only.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, words, ostinato, chords and this ABC encoding. "Take Five" (Paul Desmond) and the Dave Brubeck Quartet's recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included.
% dossier-respect: Credit Paul Desmond as composer, not only the bandleader, and name the Quartet (Dave Brubeck, Paul Desmond, Eugene Wright, Joe Morello) and the album Time Out (1959). Discuss unusual metre as a choice jazz musicians made, without calling it exotic; note that metres of five and other groupings are ordinary in many musical traditions.
% dossier-review: Grade 6 classroom music, band or choir teacher, jazz educator, second score reader and student trial; all reviews still required
